# Line endings, so that a checksum of this package means something.
#
# This repository is checked out with core.autocrlf=true and had no attributes
# file, so the working tree ended up mixed: files written by tooling kept LF,
# files git checked out were converted to CRLF, and `git archive` converted
# everything to CRLF on export. Three trees from one commit, with three
# different sets of bytes.
#
# The consequence was not cosmetic. The tarball's SHA-256 depended on which of
# those trees it was packed from, which means it identified a directory rather
# than a commit, and approving a package by its checksum quietly proved nothing.
#
# `text eol=lf` normalises every text file to LF in the repository and in the
# working tree on every platform, so a build from a fresh clone on Windows,
# macOS or a Linux runner produces the same archive. That is what makes the
# published artifact checkable against what was reviewed.
#
# Nothing here changes behaviour: Node, npm and TypeScript read either ending.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, and must never be converted.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.tgz binary
*.zip binary
